      "id": 1,
      "text": "Chical began as a satellite farming outpost of the Tiwa-speaking Isleta Pueblo, extending the Pueblo’s agricultural reach along the Rio Grande.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/chical_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Spanish explorer Juan de Oñate named the main pueblo 'Isleta' in 1598, meaning 'little island' because of its lava-flow setting.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/chical_new_mexico",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Chical experiences a cold semi-arid climate, with July highs up to 95°F and January lows near 20°F.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/chical_new_mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town sits on flat to gently rolling terrain, forming part of the Albuquerque Basin within the Rio Grande rift.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/chical_new_mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Covering about 0.8 square miles, Chical remains predominantly agricultural, with residents tending small farms and grazing livestock.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/chical_new_mexico",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "While Chical has no permanent water bodies, it lies just a few miles from the Rio Grande flood plain that stretches 2–4 miles wide.",
